course: world
lesson: 105
title: Alarms, stopwatches and how long a second is
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A second is about as long as it takes to say "one Mississippi" or "one elephant" at a calm speed. 60 seconds make a minute and 60 minutes make an hour, so a minute is sixty of those little "Mississippis".
explain: A stopwatch measures short times very exactly, even tenths and hundredths of a second. In a race, half a second is a big gap: if Lola runs 60 metres in 12.4 seconds and her friend in 11.9, the friend is 0.5 seconds faster.
explain: A timer counts down and beeps at zero. On a microwave or a phone timer, two numbers with a colon usually mean minutes and seconds, not hours and minutes: 1:30 is one and a half minutes, 90 seconds.
explain: An alarm wakes you at a set time. Snoozing gives a few more minutes, but the sleep is broken and many people feel more tired. Better to add up your morning jobs and pick one alarm that leaves time to spare.
fact: There are 60 seconds in a minute and 60 minutes in an hour.
fact: Counting "one Mississippi, two Mississippi" or "one elephant, two elephant" slows you down to about one second for each number.
fact: A stopwatch is a clock you start and stop to measure how long something takes, often to a tenth or hundredth of a second.
fact: In a race the lower time wins: 11.9 seconds beats 12.4 seconds by 0.5 seconds, half a second.
fact: The 100-metre world record, 9.58 seconds, was set by Usain Bolt in 2009; a fast 9-year-old might take about 16 to 18 seconds.
fact: A countdown timer counts down to zero and then beeps, so you do not have to guess.
fact: On a microwave or timer, 1:30 means 1 minute 30 seconds, which is 90 seconds.
fact: Half a minute is 30 seconds, so 35 seconds is just over half a minute and 25 seconds short of a whole minute.
fact: Dentists say to brush your teeth for about 2 minutes, which is 120 seconds; 30 seconds is only a quarter of that.
fact: Cooking needs the right time: a 4-minute egg taken out after 2 minutes is still runny and raw.
fact: Snoozing an alarm gives broken, light sleep, and many people feel more tired after lots of snoozing.
fact: To choose an alarm time, add up how long each morning job takes and count on from the alarm.
fact: Words people use: "ages" means a long time, "in your dreams" means no way, "mid" means just average, nothing special, "no cap" means "no lie" and "I'm dead" means "that's so funny".
exercise: How many seconds are in a minute? | 60 | 100 | 10 | 24
exercise: Max sets alarms at 6:50, 7:00 and 7:10. How long from the first to the last? | 20 minutes | 10 minutes | 30 minutes | 3 minutes
exercise: Lola runs 60 metres in 12.4 seconds; her friend runs it in 11.9. Who is faster, and by how much? | The friend, by 0.5 seconds | Lola, by 0.5 seconds | The friend, by 5 seconds | Lola, by 1.5 seconds
exercise: Dan "counts to 60" in 10 seconds in hide-and-seek. About how long should counting to 60 take? | About 1 minute | About 10 seconds | About 6 seconds | About 10 minutes
exercise: Which helps you count real seconds? | Saying "one Mississippi, two Mississippi" | Counting as fast as you can | Counting only the even numbers | Clapping your hands very quickly
exercise: Gran's egg needs 4 minutes. Rosie takes it out after 2. How much time was missing? | 2 minutes, half the time | 1 minute, a quarter of the time | 4 minutes, all of the time | 3 minutes, most of the time
exercise: Will's timer started at 10:00. It now says 8:30. How long has he worked? | 1 minute 30 seconds | 8 minutes 30 seconds | 1 hour 30 minutes | 2 minutes 30 seconds
exercise: Sofia holds her breath for 35 seconds. How far is that from a whole minute? | 25 seconds short | 5 seconds short | 35 seconds short | It is a whole minute
exercise: What is half a minute? | 30 seconds | 50 seconds | 15 seconds | 60 seconds
exercise: What is the 100-metre world record set by Usain Bolt in 2009? | 9.58 seconds | 10.58 seconds | 8.95 seconds | 12.4 seconds
exercise: Esme's microwave says 1:30. What does it mean? | 1 minute 30 seconds | 1 hour 30 minutes | 13 minutes | 130 seconds
exercise: How many seconds is 1:30 on a microwave? | 90 | 130 | 30 | 150
exercise: Kai brushes his teeth for 30 seconds instead of 2 minutes. What part of the time did he brush? | A quarter | A half | A third | Three quarters
exercise: How many seconds are in 2 minutes? | 120 | 200 | 60 | 100
exercise: Hamza wakes at 7:15 and needs 10 minutes to dress, 15 to eat and 5 to brush his teeth. When is he ready? | 7:45 | 7:50 | 7:30 | 8:00
exercise: Hamza's bus is at 7:50 and he is ready at 7:45. How many minutes spare does he have? | 5 | 10 | 15 | 0
exercise: Why do many people feel more tired after lots of snoozing? | The extra sleep is broken and not restful | Snoozing makes the night shorter by hours | Alarms use up the body's energy | Snoozing makes you sleep twice as deep
test: Max, groaning: "Snooze is life, bro." He slept through two of his three alarms. What does the science say? | Snooze sleep is broken, so he may feel more tired | Snoozing gives the best sleep of the night | Three alarms make you feel more rested | Snoozing makes the morning longer
test: Lola: "My 12.4 is mid, but you only beat me by like five seconds." Her friend ran 11.9. Put it right. | It was only half a second | It really was five seconds | It was one and a half seconds | It was four seconds exactly
test: Dan yells, "Done, counted to 60!" after 10 seconds. Everyone shouts, "Cheater!" Why? | Counting to 60 properly takes about a minute | You should only count to 10 in hide-and-seek | Counting to 60 takes about 10 minutes | Nobody can count to 60 in a game
test: Rosie, starving: "Sod it, the egg's been in 2 minutes, it'll do." The egg needed 4. What happens? | It is still runny and raw inside | It is perfectly cooked already | It is hard and burnt | It cooks faster once out of the water
test: Will moans, "This homework is taking ages!" His timer went from 10:00 to 8:30. What's the truth? | Only a minute and a half has passed | Eight and a half minutes have passed | An hour and a half has passed | Ten minutes have passed
test: Jack timed Sofia at 35 seconds: "Nearly a minute, no cap!" Is it? | No, it is just over half a minute | Yes, 35 seconds is almost a minute | Yes, a minute is 40 seconds | No, it is under half a minute
test: Theo: "I could run 100 metres in 10 seconds." Dad: "In your dreams, kid." Why is Dad right? | The best runners in history only just beat 10 seconds | Children are not allowed to run 100 metres | 10 seconds is the speed of an average child | Dad is wrong; most children can do it
test: Esme, a bit delulu (believing something silly): "Microwave says 1:30, popcorn's gonna take an hour and a half." What's the truth? | It is 1 minute 30 seconds | It is 1 hour 30 minutes | It is 13 minutes | It is 130 seconds
test: What would happen if Hamza set his alarm 10 minutes later, at 7:25, and did the same jobs? | He would be ready at 7:55 and miss the 7:50 bus | He would be ready at 7:45 with time to spare | He would be ready at exactly 7:50 | He would be ready at 7:35, very early
test: A cartoon hero says, "Count to three Mississippis, that's three minutes!" Put it right. | It is about three seconds | It is exactly three minutes | It is about thirty seconds | It is about three hours
test: Kai's sand timer is set for 2 minutes. He finishes in 30 seconds. "I'm dead, that was fast!" How many more seconds should he have brushed? | 90 seconds | 30 seconds | 60 seconds | 120 seconds
exam: Determine the number of seconds comprised in two minutes. | One hundred and twenty | Two hundred | Sixty | One hundred
exam: It is given that a microwave display reads 1:30. Ascertain the duration thereby indicated. | One minute and thirty seconds | One hour and thirty minutes | Thirteen minutes | One hundred and thirty seconds
exam: Whereas one competitor records 12.4 seconds and another 11.9 seconds, identify the faster competitor and the margin. | The latter, by half a second | The former, by half a second | The latter, by five seconds | The former, by one and a half seconds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the world record for one hundred metres set in 2009? | It stands at 9.58 seconds, achieved by Usain Bolt | It stands at 10.58 seconds, achieved by Usain Bolt | It stands at 9.58 minutes, achieved by Usain Bolt | It stands at 12.4 seconds, achieved by a child
